    "complementary": {
      "label": "Complementary",
      "readings": [
        {
          "role": "first",
          "ref": "Isaiah 1:10-18",
          "refKey": "Isa.1.10-Isa.1.18",
          "tags": {
            "theme": [
              "justice",
              "mercy-and-forgiveness",
              "prayer-and-worship",
              "repentance"
            ],
            "image": [
              "blood"
            ],
            "mood": [
              "prophetic-warning"
            ],
            "function": [
              "confession",
              "pardon-assurance"
            ]
          },
          "notes": [
            {
              "chapter": 1,
              "v_start": 10,
              "v_end": 10,
              "text": "Of Sodom — So called for their resemblance of them in wickedness. The law - The message which I am now to deliver to you from God, your great lawgiver. 11. To me - Who am a spirit, and therefore cannot be satisfied with such carnal oblations, but expect to have your hearts and lives, as well as your bodies and sacrifices, presented unto me. Blood - He mentions the fat and blood, because these were in a peculiar manner reserved for God, to intimate that even the best of their sacrifices were rejected by him."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 1,
              "v_start": 12,
              "v_end": 12,
              "text": "To appear — Upon the three solemn feasts, or upon other occasions. Who required - The thing I commanded, was not only, nor chiefly, that you should offer external sacrifices, but that you should do it with true repentance, with faith in my promises, and sincere resolutions of devoting yourselves to my service."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 1,
              "v_start": 13,
              "v_end": 13,
              "text": "The solemn meeting — The most solemn day of each of the three feasts, which was the last day."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 1,
              "v_start": 15,
              "v_end": 15,
              "text": "Blood — You are guilty of murder, and oppression."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 1,
              "v_start": 16,
              "v_end": 16,
              "text": "Wash — Cleanse your hearts and hands."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 1,
              "v_start": 17,
              "v_end": 17,
              "text": "Learn — Begin to live soberly, righteously, and godly. Judgment - Shew your religion to God, by practicing justice to men. Judge - Defend and deliver them."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "role": "psalm",
          "ref": "Psalm 32:1-7",
          "refKey": "Ps.32.1-Ps.32.7",
          "tags": {
            "theme": [
              "grace",
              "mercy-and-forgiveness",
              "repentance",
              "sin-and-fall"
            ],
            "image": [
              "rock-and-refuge",
              "water"
            ],
            "mood": [
              "joy",
              "penitence"
            ],
            "function": [
              "confession",
              "pardon-assurance"
            ]
          },
          "notes": [
            {
              "chapter": 32,
              "v_start": 2,
              "v_end": 2,
              "text": "Imputeth — Whom God doth not charge with the guilt of his sins, but graciously pardons and accepts him in Christ. No guile - Who freely confesses all his sins, and turns from sin to God with all his heart."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 32,
              "v_start": 3,
              "v_end": 3,
              "text": "Silence — From a full and open confession of my sins. Old - My spirit failed, and the strength of my body decayed. Roaring - Because of the continual horrors of my conscience, and sense of God's wrath."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 32,
              "v_start": 4,
              "v_end": 4,
              "text": "Hand — Thy afflicting hand. My moisture - Was dried up."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 32,
              "v_start": 5,
              "v_end": 5,
              "text": "The iniquity — The guilt of my sin."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 32,
              "v_start": 6,
              "v_end": 6,
              "text": "For this — Upon the encouragement of my example. Found - In an acceptable and seasonable time, while God continues to offer grace and mercy. Waters - In the time of great calamities. Not come - So as to overwhelm him."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "role": "second",
          "ref": "2 Thessalonians 1:1-4, 11-12",
          "refKey": "2Thess.1.1-2Thess.1.4",
          "tags": {
            "theme": [
              "faith-and-trust",
              "judgment",
              "suffering-and-endurance",
              "thanksgiving"
            ],
            "mood": [
              "assurance",
              "exhortation"
            ]
          },
          "notes": [
            {
              "chapter": 1,
              "v_start": 1,
              "v_end": 1,
              "text": "Congratulates their constancy in the faith, and exhorts them to advance daily in grace and wisdom."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 1,
              "v_start": 2,
              "v_end": 2,
              "text": "Reforms their mistake concerning the coming of our Lord And,"
            },
            {
              "chapter": 1,
              "v_start": 3,
              "v_end": 3,
              "text": "Recommends several Christian duties. The parts of it are five: I. The inscription, Chap. i. 1, 2 II. Thanksgiving and prayer for them, 3-12 III. The doctrine concerning the man of sin,. ii. 1-12 Whence he comforts them against this trial, 13, 14 Adding exhortation and prayer, 15-17 IV. An exhortation to prayer, with a prayer for. iii. 1-5 them, to correct the disorderly, 6-16 V. The conclusion, 17, 18 2nd THESSALONIANS"
            },
            {
              "chapter": 1,
              "v_start": 4,
              "v_end": 4,
              "text": "Which ye endure — \"That ye may be accounted worthy of the kingdom.\""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "role": "gospel",
          "ref": "Luke 19:1-10",
          "refKey": "Luke.19.1-Luke.19.10",
          "tags": {
            "theme": [
              "deliverance",
              "grace",
              "repentance",
              "wealth-and-possessions"
            ],
            "mood": [
              "joy"
            ],
            "function": [
              "pardon-assurance"
            ]
          },
          "notes": [
            {
              "chapter": 19,
              "v_start": 1,
              "v_end": 1,
              "text": "He passed through Jericho — So that Zacchaeus must have lived near the end of the town: the tree was in the town itself. And he was rich - These words seem to refer to the discourse in the last chapter, ver. 24, particularly to ver. 27. Zacchiaeus is a proof, that it is possible by the power of God for even a rich man to enter into the kingdom of heaven."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 19,
              "v_start": 2,
              "v_end": 2,
              "text": "The chief of the publicans — What we would term, commissioner of the customs. A very honourable as well as profitable place."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 19,
              "v_start": 4,
              "v_end": 4,
              "text": "And running before — With great earnestness. He climbed up - Notwithstanding his quality: desire conquering honour and shame."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 19,
              "v_start": 5,
              "v_end": 5,
              "text": "Jesus said, Zacchaeus, make haste and come down — What a strange mixture of passions must Zacchaeus have now felt, hearing one speak, as knowing both his name and his heart!"
            },
            {
              "chapter": 19,
              "v_start": 7,
              "v_end": 7,
              "text": "They all murmured — All who were near: though most of them rather out of surprise than indignation."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 19,
              "v_start": 8,
              "v_end": 8,
              "text": "And Zacchaeus stood — Showing by his posture, his deliberate, purpose and ready mind, and said, Behold, Lord, I give - I determine to do it immediately."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 19,
              "v_start": 9,
              "v_end": 9,
              "text": "He also is a son of Abraham — A Jew born, and as such has a right to the first offer of salvation."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 19,
              "v_start": 10,
              "v_end": 10,
              "text": "Matt. xviii, 11."
            }
          ]
        }
